### =========================================================================
###  Research Agent — the API, by hand
###
###  Click "Send Request" above any block below. The requests are in the order
###  the workshop walks through them, and the polling flow further down is the
###  whole point of the session: POST comes back instantly, GET catches up.
###
###  Point this at your deployed service by swapping which @baseUrl is live.
### =========================================================================

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000
# @baseUrl = https://your-service.onrender.com


### What is this service?
GET {{baseUrl}}/
Accept: application/json


### Is it alive, what's it running, and can it reach the database?
#
# On a free tier this is also the wake-up call. Send it ~10 minutes before a
# demo so nobody watches the cold start. `database: false` means the Supabase
# key or URL is wrong, the project has paused, or the tables don't exist yet.
#
# `provider` and `model` are how you confirm a switched-over deployment is
# really using the key you think it is. `web_search: false` means no Tavily key
# is set, so the agent has Wikipedia only and can't answer anything recent.
GET {{baseUrl}}/health
Accept: application/json


### =========================================================================
###  The right way — accept the job, poll for the result
### =========================================================================

### 1. Hand over the work. Should return 202 in ~200ms.
#
# Note what comes back: a run id and "queued". No answer. The agent has not
# started thinking yet, and that is exactly the behaviour we want — this
# response is a receipt, not a result.
# @name createRun
POST {{baseUrl}}/runs
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "query": "how do solar panels actually work?"
}


### 2. Poll for it. Send this one repeatedly.
#
# `{{createRun.response.body.$.run_id}}` reuses the id from the request above,
# so there is nothing to copy by hand — just keep hitting Send.
#
# Watch `steps` grow on each send: that array IS the agent's thinking, written
# to the database as it happens. `status` goes queued -> running -> done.
GET {{baseUrl}}/runs/{{createRun.response.body.$.run_id}}
Accept: application/json


### =========================================================================
###  Conversations — the same agent, with a memory
### =========================================================================

### 3. Ask a follow-up, in the same thread.
#
# Note the question: "why is that?" means nothing on its own. It only works
# because `thread_id` tells the server which conversation to reload — and the
# server replays the whole thing to the model, which remembers nothing by
# itself.
#
# Send request 1 above first; `thread_id` comes back in that response.
# @name followUp
POST {{baseUrl}}/runs
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "query": "why is that? explain the physics in one paragraph",
  "thread_id": "{{createRun.response.body.$.thread_id}}"
}


### Poll the follow-up.
GET {{baseUrl}}/runs/{{followUp.response.body.$.run_id}}
Accept: application/json


### 4. Read the whole conversation back.
#
# Every run in the thread, oldest first. This is what the client calls after a
# page reload — proof that the conversation lives in Postgres and not in a
# browser tab.
GET {{baseUrl}}/threads/{{createRun.response.body.$.thread_id}}
Accept: application/json


### Something recent — the question Wikipedia can't answer.
#
# Needs TAVILY_API_KEY set (check `web_search` on /health). Without it the
# agent should tell you plainly that it can't check current information,
# rather than confidently answering from its training data.
POST {{baseUrl}}/runs
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "query": "what AI model releases were announced this month?"
}


### =========================================================================
###  The wrong way — kept so you can feel the problem
### =========================================================================

### The naive endpoint: the agent runs inside the request.
#
# ⚠️  This blocks for 30-60 seconds and your HTTP client may well give up
#     before the server does — which is precisely the failure this workshop is
#     about. On localhost it usually survives. Deployed behind a proxy, this is
#     the request that becomes a 504 with no way to recover.
POST {{baseUrl}}/runs/naive
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "query": "what is the photoelectric effect?"
}


### =========================================================================
###  The guardrails
### =========================================================================

### Too short — 422, and it costs zero model calls.
#
# FastAPI rejects this from the type hints alone, before any handler runs.
# Validation is a quota guardrail, not just a correctness one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/runs
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "query": "hi"
}


### Unknown run — 404, not a 500.
#
# Two shapes worth checking, because they fail in different places: an id that
# isn't a uuid at all, and one that is a valid uuid but matches no row.
GET {{baseUrl}}/runs/does-not-exist
Accept: application/json

###
GET {{baseUrl}}/runs/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Accept: application/json
